+Set `FT_DEV_EXPERT_POLICY=profile` and `FT_DEV_EXPERT_PROFILE=PROFILE.strp` to preload ranked experts.  With prefill overlap, slots `[0, 2E)` are reserved for the volatile double buffers, so the profile always starts at `2E`.  The code records profile hits, misses and replacement bytes.  `profile+adapt` stays fail-closed until the scheduler-owned `RuntimeAdaptationCoordinator` has stopped launch, drained the actual completion and copy streams, and issued a proof bound to scheduler identity, launch generation, cache/device, mapping generation, boundary sequence and private key.
+
+`FT_DEV_EXPERT_ADAPT_LAG1=1` is default-off.  It freezes a plan at boundary N, excludes all stable slots referenced by N's raw top-k routes, copies each replacement to private staging on an auxiliary copy stream, and does not change `slot_for_id`, `id_of_slot`, or a live cache row at N.  At a later boundary the cache first observes that transaction's completion event; an incomplete event leaves the old map and blocks a second pending transaction.  Only a complete event permits staged rows and mappings to publish. Protection covers every layer in a scheduler step, including a previously missing expert allocated by native LRU; a current-step conflict discards the staged transaction. Private staging does not reserve a live slot against LRU. Enabled lag-one mode disables CUDA graph capture so host protection is re-executed on every step.  The transaction keeps per-swap D0 receipts (step, boundary, layer, expert, slot, byte count, enqueue-start, enqueue-finish, observed completion time, and publication time) in `${FT_DEV_D0_PATH:-/tmp/ft_dev_d0}_adaptation.jsonl`.  Enqueue and observed completion are deliberately distinct fields. The step is the scheduler boundary sequence; the per-layer LRU clock is recorded separately. Synchronous adaptation emits the same identity and timing fields. Transfer bytes include copied but discarded replacements; committed swap bytes count published rows. D0-off creates no per-swap diagnostic receipts or timing events.
+
+CUDA publication still requires the existing scheduler-owned runtime proof.  CPU fixtures model the protocol with controllable test-completion events; those events do not represent GPU completion and do not prove CUDA stream ordering or token equivalence.  A failed publication restores rows and both mapping directions; a failed rollback locks only the opt-in policy.
 
